# Protocol baseline
#
# The upstream snapshot this client implementation is written against. Every
# protocol-layer change must be traceable to the version recorded here.
# Drift detection (scripts/drift-check.sh) reads this file.

UPSTREAM_REPO=NodePassProject/Nowhere
UPSTREAM_BRANCH=main
UPSTREAM_TAG=v3.0.1
UPSTREAM_COMMIT=00000e0
UPSTREAM_COMMIT_DATE=2026-10-16
BINARY_VERSION_STRING=nowhere-v3.0.1
ALPN=nw3

# Normative files watched for change. compatibility.md was deleted in v3;
# interoperability.md is new and states what a compatible peer must do.
SPEC_FILES=docs/protocol.md docs/configuration.md docs/security.md docs/interoperability.md

# Known state
# - Advanced from v1.8.2 (05366539) to v3.0.1 (00000e0) on 2026-10-21. The first
#   drift to reach the wire, and it reached nearly all of it. Unlike the two
#   advances below this is not a transcription fix: it is a different protocol
#   under the same name, and this client was rewritten to it. Decided as D-21
#   (internal); the reasoning in brief:
#
#     * ALPN `now/1` -> `nw3`, fixed; exporter label EXPORTER-Nowhere-nw3-Auth.
#     * AuthFrame: session_id[16] || tag[16] -> tag[16] alone, over
#       transport || exporter, with separate Vector and Egress role keys from
#       salt SHA256("nowhere/nw3/auth-root").
#     * FlowHeader (5 bytes) -> FlowStart (1 byte, bit 0 = UDP).
#     * SetupResult renumbered and shrunk to five values.
#     * TLS Mux, split carriers and session pairing removed. TLS is one
#       connection per flow; QUIC is one connection for all of them.
#     * QUIC UDP flow ids derived from the stream id, (id >> 2) + 1, and the
#       DATAGRAM header became a 4-byte base word with a 2-bit type.
#     * Morph, new and on by default: ChaCha20 masking below TLS (64-byte
#       prelude + 12-byte nonce) and below QUIC (12-byte nonce per datagram).
#     * No unverified mode: system trust and hostname, or a pin.
#     * Share links: `net=tcp|udp` (default tcp) and `morph=0|1`; keys are 32
#       lowercase hex characters.
#     * Target encoding, UoT and fragment reassembly unchanged.
#
#   Between the v3.0.1 tag and main at eee7965c the SPEC_FILES are identical
#   (the later commits reorganise source only), so the pin is the tag.
#
#   Verified at v3.0.1, on Linux x86_64, with the oracle built by
#   `cargo build --release --locked`:
#     * verify-vectors.py: 86 checks, including upstream's own vectors.json,
#       its Morph key fixture and RFC 8439's ChaCha20 vector;
#     * smoke-local.sh: SOCKS5 through the oracle's Vector over TLS and QUIC;
#     * oracle-diff.sh: this client and the oracle agree on every case over
#       TLS and QUIC, each with Morph on and off, and on connection counts;
#     * portal-lifecycle.sh: reclaim at the handshake deadline (5.0 s, answered
#       INVALID_REQUEST) and recovery across a Portal restart;
#     * quic-probe.sh: ngtcp2 + aws-lc export keying material a v3 Portal
#       accepts;
#     * on an API 35 emulator: the instrumentation suite, e2e-tunnel-fetch.sh
#       and e2e-fakeip.sh over both carriers.
#
#   Two behaviours only the live suite found, recorded because both look like
#   the client's fault and neither is a matter of reading the spec harder:
#     * "Target payload waits for READY" is enforced: a Portal watching the
#       stream while it dials ends the flow if a byte arrives early. v1 allowed
#       the first payload in the opening write, and this client used to send it
#       there.
#     * A refused AuthFrame closes the connection rather than leaving it
#       silent. Over QUIC that arrived as a connection error rather than an end
#       of stream, and the oracle reads it as "no setup result".
#
# Earlier history, for the record of how the tracking policy came to be.
#
# - Advanced from 8807960c to 05366539 on 2026-08-29. One commit, "Correct
#   documented runtime defaults", changing two lines of docs/security.md: the
#   TLS Mux shard density 12 -> 4, in prose and in an ASCII diagram.
#
#   It is a documentation correction rather than a change. protocol.md has said
#   4 since v1.8.2 and security.md still said 12; upstream made the two agree.
#   This client already implements 4 -- MuxHeader.SHARD_FLOW_THRESHOLD, and the
#   fixture pins it -- so nothing here moves, and the reason the pin moves at
#   all is that a baseline naming a commit whose documents disagreed with each
#   other is a worse record than one naming the commit where they stopped.
#
#   Verified before moving: 45 vector checks pass, smoke-local.sh carries
#   traffic end to end against the oracle rebuilt at 05366539, and no wire
#   field, layout, length, ordering or derivation is touched by the diff.
# - Advanced from v1.8.0 to v1.8.2 on 2026-08-26, on the first normative change
#   the drift check has ever reported. What changed, in full:
#     * TLS Mux shard density 12 -> 4 active flows before another shard opens
#       (protocol.md section 7, compatibility.md, configuration.md, operations.md);
#     * the default NOW_QUIC_MEMORY_PROFILE moved from `balanced` to `throughput`,
#       which is a Portal-side environment default with no client-visible effect.
#   Both are runtime tuning. No wire field, frame layout, length, ordering or
#   derivation changed anywhere: the diff over 4 commits touches one Rust
#   constant, one env default, their tests, and the prose that quotes them.
#   Every byte-level vector in this suite is unaffected, and re-running
#   verify-vectors.py after the transcription fix confirms it.
# - Sharding is client-side placement (protocol.md: "Sharding is runtime placement
#   and does not add wire fields"), so a client using the old density would still
